"All ten expert ratings call the race Solid/Safe R", "Recent polls show Bengs within single digits but sponsored by Democratic-leaning pollsters"], "rationale": "Rounds combines incumbency, a massive money advantage, and a state that leans Republican by fifteen points, and every expert rater places the race in the Safe or Solid R column. Bengs' independent bid and some late Democratic-sponsored polls showing a closer race justify a modestly narrower expected margin than the fundamentals alone, but not a competitive one.", "watch": "A late surge in Bengs' name recognition or a major Rounds ethics scandal could tighten the race, though the state's partisan floor remains very high.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Mike Rounds (R) is running.", "Brian Bengs is an independent standing in for the Democratic side."]}, "overview": "Senator Mike Rounds seeks reelection in a state that favors Republicans by about fifteen points, facing independent Brian Bengs, who is running as the de facto challenger after the Democratic nominee withdrew. Rounds holds a commanding financial edge, with roughly four times Bengs' fundraising and substantial outside support, while Bengs has spent most of what he raised.\n\nEvery major race rater calls the contest Safe or Solid Republican, and prediction markets price Bengs' chances near zero. A few Democratic-sponsored polls from late summer showed the gap narrowing to low single digits, but earlier surveys and the state's partisan baseline point to a comfortable Rounds win, with Bengs' independent positioning adding some uncertainty to the exact margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0278, "margin": -11.24}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0347, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0268, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0205, "margin": -13.0}]}